exercises.solution:
```python
def forall(domain, pred):
    return all(pred(a) for a in domain)

def exists(domain, pred):
    return any(pred(a) for a in domain)

def serial(domain, rel):
    return forall(domain, lambda x: exists(domain, lambda y: (x, y) in rel))

def has_hub(domain, rel):
    return exists(domain, lambda y: forall(domain, lambda x: (x, y) in rel))
```

This is Tarski's definition as code: $\forall$ is `all` over the domain and $\exists$ is `any`. The tests on the empty domain show why standard first-order logic insists on non-empty structures: there $\forall x\, \varphi$ is always true and $\exists x\, \varphi$ always false, so $\forall x\, \varphi \to \exists x\, \varphi$ would fail. On infinite domains the loops never finish, which is why truth in infinite structures needs proof rather than search.
